1 NAME Benajah /Brown/
2 GIVN Benajah
2 SURN Brown
2 _MARNM Brown
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 ADDR
3 CITY Ingersol Cemetery
3 STAE Ontario
3 CTRY Canada
1 BIRT
2 DATE 23 OCT 1762
2 PLAC Lebanon Twp., New London,Connecticut, USA
2 ADDR
1 DEAT
2 DATE 25 JAN 1805
2 PLAC Burlington Bay, Wentworth County,  Upper Canada
2 ADDR
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} 
2 CONT He married at Dutchess County, New York, 8 September 1784, VIOLETTA PAINE, daughter of Lt. Colonel Brinton and Hannah (Hills) Paine.[ ] She was born at Dutchess County, New York, 16 September 1767 and died at the home of her daugh
2 CONC ter, Electa Harper at Malahide Township, Elgin, County, Ontario, 15 October 1844.[ ] Her gravestone at the Burdick Cemetery was erected by her grandsons in 1900.[ ] After Benajah drowned Violetta married next Solomon Nichol and ha
2 CONC d two chilren, Sylvenus and Samuel. After Solomon died in 1813 Violetta married third Paul Dusten who died in 1822.
2 CONT
2 CONT Benajah served during the Revolutionary War, but immigrated to Upper Canada in 1797 after losing his land in New York through civil action.
2 CONT
2 CONT Cayuga County, New York land records at Auburn, New York indicate a sale of land by Edward Paine (Violetta's Uncle Edward, the founder of Painesville, Ohio) on 16 August 1794 at the "Town of Scipio, County of Onondaga [now Cayuga 
2 CONC County] and State of New York, Esquire of the one part and Benajah Brown of the place aforesaid of the other Part. Witnessth, That the said Edward Paine for and in consideration of the sum of Thirty six Pounds sixteen shillings la
2 CONC wful money of the State of New York, to him in hand paid...Doth grant... that certain Lot Piece or parcel of Land situated Lying and being in the County of Onondago Being a part of Lot Number Fifty four in the Town of Scipio agree
2 CONC able to a Survey made by the Surveyor General of Said State and Filed in the Secretaries Office of Said State agreeable to law Beginning at the Northeast Corner of Said Lot and runs from thence West fifty three Chaines thence sout
2 CONC h to a large Brook thence Easterly up said Brook to the East line of Said Lot Fifty four thence North along said line to the Place of Beginning Containing by estimation Ninety two Acres of Land be the same move on Less Together wi
2 CONC th all and ..." It is signed by Edward Paine and witnessed by Edward's brother Timothy Paine and by Jasen Thurston. Benajah had witnessed a sale of land by Edward Paine just before his sale. The land sale was allowed to be recorde
2 CONC d by Seth Phelps, the first Judge of the Court of Common Pleas in and for the County of Onondaga.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1790 at Chemung Township, Montgomery County [as it was in 1790] on page 105, is Benajah Brown, with 2 males 16 and over, 2 males under 16, and 2 females. Living near him is Jonathan Brown with one male over 16, one male under 1
2 CONC 6 and one female in the household. This doesn't fit perfectly with Benajah' known family. He would have had two daughters, Elizabeth, 4 or 5, Clarissa, 1, his wife Violetta, and himself. Possibly the youngest was recorded as a boy
2 CONC . There is also another male over 16 and another male under 16 indicated? The fit above for Conradt Smith of 1 male over 16, and 3 females is a perfect fit for Benajah. Is it possible that the data was miss-recorded?
2 CONT
2 CONT Benajah Brown is listed as soldier in the 2nd Connecticut Regiment, Short Term Levies, June 25, 1779. He is from Hebron, (Rolls and Lists of Connecticut Men in the Revolution, 1775-1783) [1901 Hartford Connecticut Historical Socie
2 CONC ty; copy at Thatcher Graduate Library, Ann Arbor, MI] There are 10 slips for him for service between 1779 and 1780; a private; 2nd Conn. Regmt, commanded by Capt. Erastus Wolcutt;s Co., @nd Regmt by Col. Zebulon Butler.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Upper Canada Land Petition for Benajah Brown was signed by him at York, Upper Canada, 11 August 1801. It indicates that "he has been upwards of two years in the Province, that he has taken the Oaths, and being in a situation t
2 CONC o cultivate lands prays yr. Excellecncy will please to grant him 200" acres of land. A supporting document certifies "That he has a wife and six children - That he is an earnest, industrious well behaved man and that he possesses 
2 CONC stock and the implements of Husbandry necessary to carry on the business of farming."[ ]
2 CONT
2 CONT Major sources for information on the ancestors of Benajah Brown are: Ancestry and posterity of Brinton Paine Brown, by Enoch Burdick Brown, Brownsville, Ontario, 1904 A short sketch of the life of Brinton Paine Brown (Senior), by 
2 CONC Enoch Burdick Brown, Brownsville, Ontario, 1904. [This comes from E. B. Brown's books which Edna (Young) Johnson copied several times]
2 CONT
2 CONT "Violetta Paine Violetta, 3rd daughter of Col. Brinton Paine Col. Brinton was born Sept. 16th 1767 at New York (Other records indicate that she was born at Connecticut-BCJ). She married Benajah Brown, born Oct. 23rd 1762 of Scotti
2 CONC sh parents which is all we seem to know of him. They were married on Sept. 8, 1784. I believe Benajah was a captain in the Winchester Militia, 4th regiment but there seems to be some confusion about this record. [The 4th reg. had 
2 CONC a Capt. Benajah Brown who died in 1822 and a Benajah Brown who was a private---BCJ] At the close of the revolutionary war, Benajah Brown bought 600 acres of land on the Genesee River at Painted Post and had been living on this lan
2 CONC d several years when a General Wadsworth claimed it as his. They went to court about it and Benajah was dispossessed and financially ruined. He was very angry naturally, and declared that if the country for which he had fought wou
2 CONC ldn't protect him in his honest rights he wouldn't stay in it so in 1797, in the Fall, he brought his family into Canada crossing the Niagara River at Fort Erie. For the winter he stayed at Fort Erie and worked at odd jobs while V
2 CONC ioletta earned a little knitting socks for the soldiers stationed there. Then he went to a farm, just north of Brantford on Grand R., now known as Bow Park farm and run as an experimental farm for Canadian Canners Ltd. [This was w
2 CONC ritten in 1942]. While there the Indian Chief Joseph Brant asked him to teach Indians how to farm and he tried to do so. But Violetta didn't like it. She protested against raising her family entirely amongst Indians so in 1801 the
2 CONC y moved to Oxford County and took up land on the Thames River about two miles west of present town of Ingersol. In 1805 [late December 1804] Benajah Brown and a neighbor, Major Tousley, went to Little York (Toronto) to get deeds f
2 CONC or their lands. On their way home they undertook a short cut across Burlington Bay on the ice but had gone only a short distance when the ice gave way. People on shore came to help and Benajah said, "Help the Major first: I can ke
2 CONC ep up." So a rope was thrown to the Major and he was pulled out. Then it was thrown to Benajah but they jerked it so hard and quick that it broke and weighted by a heavy and by this time thoroughly soaked bearskin coat, he went do
2 CONC wn and did not come up. He was grappled out and buried in Ingersol cemetery on [King St.] [Bible indicates he died Jan. 25th 1805]. Violetta was left in a new and sparsely settled country with a large young family so she lost no t
2 CONC ime in remarrying again. Later in 1806 she married Solomon Nichols. They had two sons Sylvanus born 1807 and Samuel 1809. The farm near Ingersol was sold and she and her family and Solomon moved to Norfolk Co. to a farm about two 
2 CONC miles West of Port Rowan. Solomon died in 1814. Violetta lived on at her home for many years but she finally sold it and went to live with her children. She died in 1844 at the home of her daughter Electa (Mrs. Samuel Harper) who 
2 CONC lived a few miles east of Aylmer and she is buried in the Burdick cemetery there. Of the children of Violetta and Benajah, other than Walter who was my great-grandfather I know very little. The man, Enoch Brown, who wrote the fami
2 CONC ly history which I mentioned in the beginning of this story was the son of Brinton Brown who took up land at 'Brownsville', north east of Aylmer. The village was named for him. At different times I have known my mother's cousins f
2 CONC rom this branch of the family but never very intimately. Cora Chase has always kept in close touch."
2 CONT
2 CONT Upper Canada Land Petitions, N Misc./ 7, 1798, Long Point
2 CONT
2 CONT To his honour Peter Russell Esquire, President of the ? of his Majesty's Province of Upper Canada In council The humble Petition of Solomon Nicholes Most Humbly Showeth That your Petitioner has Served on board of His Majesty's Shi
2 CONC p Royal Oak for the space of three years. Your Petitioner came to this province and resided in the township of Woodhouse in the County of Norfolk this two years past.
2 CONT
2 CONT Requested two hundred acres of land.
2 CONT
2 CONT New York in the Revolution, by Berthold Fernow, 1972 Gen 974.7 D659, v. 15 c.2 Texas State Library, Austin, TX, p. 307
2 CONT
2 CONT Levies and Militia, Westchester County, Lewis Morris, Brig. Gen., June 25, 17778 Fourth Regiment Capt. Ebenezer Scofield's Co. divided into 2 and offered as follows; Benj'n Brown, 1st Lieut. (named rectified to Benijah Brown, June
2 CONC  16, 1780, and April 10, 1782, Supplement 1901, p. 212- Land Bounty Rights - Benajah Brown, Certificates of the Surveyor General.
2 CONT
2 CONT Pierce's Register of the Certificates Issued by John Pierce, Esq. Paymaster General and Commissioner of the Army Accounts for the US to officers and soldiers of the Continental Army, shows: Certif # 47861 Brown, Benajah $20.42 Cer
2 CONC tif #48228 Brown, Benajah $60.00
2 CONT
2 CONT Pension Records in the National Archives, of the United States Benajah Brown - W.21021 A letter of 2 September 1936 to Mrs. Edward D. Brown of Tecumseh, Michigan summarizes the material found in Benajah's pension file.
2 CONT
2 CONT "Reference is made to your letter in which you request information in regard to Benajah Brown who was born October 23, 1762, served in the Revolutionary War and died January 25, 1805. The data which follow were obtained from paper
2 CONC s on file in pension claim, W.21021, based upon the service of Benajah Brown in the War of the Revolution. Benajah Brown was born October 23, 1762, place not stated, nor are the names of his parents shown. His father and family we
2 CONC re living in Westchester County, New York during the Revolution. While residing in Westchester County, New York, Benajah Brown volunteered sometime in August, 1778, served as sergeant and lieutenant in Captain Ebenezer Scofield's 
2 CONC and Samuel Lewis' companies, Colone 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tweedsmuir Histories - Capt. Benajah Brown
2 CONT
2 CONT Transcribed by Mike Brown 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sketch of trip through Catfish Creek to Brownsville in 1841 by the Brown family
2 CONT
2 CONT Brownsville Memorial Celebration Must Wait
2 CONT
2 CONT "Where in the world are you taking us?" Stout-hearted Mrs. Brinton Paine Brown was in tears.   Ten year old Enoch set to drive them over the 100 rod wide Catfish marsh (near Corinth).   The frightened horses threatened to bolt as 
2 CONC each log section of the "floating road" submerged at the touch of hooves! "Why mother," cheered her preaching Methodist husband, coming up with his ox-drawn load, "we’re crossing Jordan. Canaan is just ahead, full of milk and hone
2 CONC y.   There may be Anaks and Amelikites, but we’ll drive them out."
2 CONT
2 CONT That was in 1841 when the Browns came to Brownsville, says A. S. Paragus in the St. Thomas Times-Journal.   And various other pioneers at around the same time.   Little did they dream that Anaks and Amelikites, risen up over the e
2 CONC arth, would prevent their descendents celebrating the centenary (1941-42) of the founding of the settlement.   For Brownsville had planned a memorial fete to revive local history – an old boys, perhaps, with Prof. Landon to unveil
2 CONC  a cairn in honor of the pioneers.   But with so many of the young people away, celebration has been postponed for the duration.
2 CONT
2 CONT If the families of the Nims, Hopkins, Woods, other pioneers, can produce as much story as the late Enoch Burdick Brown was able to gather about the Browns, such an anniversary would ramify through a wide swath of history – from 16
2 CONC 38 to the present.
2 CONT
2 CONT Memorial cairns do not bother much with women pioneers, but if one knows the story of the leading women of a family, one pretty well knows all.   Violetta Paine, born in Bolton, Conn., can never have dreamed where her marriage to 
2 CONC Capt. Benajah Brown of the Winchester militia would lead her and her descendants.   Violetta was the daughter of Col. Brinton Paine of the Revolutionary Army, later a member of the New York Legislature, first judge of Tioga County
2 CONC .   Her uncle was Gen. Paine, for whom Painsville, Ohio, erected a statue.   Tradition even claims that Tom Paine, whose pamphlet, "Common Sense" is now considered the key that turned the American Revolution, was of the same famil
2 CONC y.   Violetta’s husband took up 600 acres at Genesco, N.Y., occupied the tract for several years, when Gen. Wadsworth claimed possession, and having influence, dispossessed Cap. Brown.
2 CONT
2 CONT Incensed that the country for which he had fought would not support the honest rights of a citizen, Capt. Benajah shook its dust from his feet, moved his family to Canada (1797); and the niece of the American Gen. Paine, knit sock
2 CONC s for the British garrison at Fort Erie.   Later, Capt. Brown under Chief Brant, taught agriculture to the Indians on Grand River.   Finally the Browns, homesteaded west of Ingersoll.   It was when returning from Little York with 
2 CONC the deed that Capt. Brown’s team went through the ice in Burlington Bay and he was drowned.   Violetta sold the Ingersoll farm, moved her nine children to a farm east of Port Rowan, a more cultural district.   She married Samuel N
2 CONC ichols.   The lady died (1844) at the home of a daughter, Mrs. Sam Harper, Malahide, and rests in the Burdick Cemetery east of Aylmer.
2 CONT
2 CONT Elizabeth Hoy was another woman who, like her mother-in-law, Violetta, was to realize strange adventures from the Brown quality of rapid, dynamic decision.   Elizabeth married Brinton Paine Brown, born at Genesco N.Y., 1797, and e
2 CONC ducated at Port Rowan.   The couple had just settled on 200 acres west of Courtland, now called old Ronson homestead, had two acres cleared, when her husband came in from the woods one morning, brought the ox-sled to the door and 
2 CONC requested Elizabeth to pack up!   The startled bride protested.   But Brinton Paine Brown had suddenly decided he might work tow lifetimes clearing off these pines and their stumps "and the deed wouldn’t hold the land from blowing
2 CONC  away."
2 CONT
2 CONT After a period of working on shares in Walsingham, Brinton Paine Brown had another inspiration.   He, Abram Smith, a brother-in-law, young Burnham, each drew 100 acres in Sombra Township on the St. Clair River, nearly 300 miles by
2 CONC  water.   They built a "Durham" boat, laid a floor, bunks in it, canvassed the front over, brought on the women, the five children.   With sail and oar, hugging the land, they reached the mouth of the Detroit River when a very bla
2 CONC ck storm arose.   They steered for a house on shore.   The water became so shallow that M. Brown had to wade some distance, carrying the women and children to land.
2 CONT
2 CONT While the men sought a place to tie the boat, the women approached the house.   The owner, a French-Canadian Catholic, refused to entertain Protestants, allowing them only a leaky, straw-roofed stable for shelter, selling them mil
2 CONC k, bread and butter for the pittance of cash they had.   The anguish of the next 36 hours was never forgotten by those women.   The boat had disappeared in the open lake; the men did not return.   All the next day they took turns 
2 CONC watching the shore and being cursed by the Frenchman.   What could they do?   Walk back home with tow babes in arms, no bridges, roads, money, provisions?   Impossible!   In despair they could only pray.   As for the men in the bo
2 CONC at, beaten to the open lake by the storm, they could only run before the wind.   Smith and Burnham, seasick, lay down in their bunks to die.   But Brinton Paine Brown stuck by the helm, and finally glimpsed a small island, steered
2 CONC  in its lee and tied up in a cove.   His companions refused to leave from the shelter until the lake was calm and it was the second morning before they got to shore and relieved the tension of the women.
2 CONT
2 CONT And when at last they reached their claims, Brinton Paine Brown found his to be mostly black ash swamp and nearly half a mile from the river!   They stuck it out until the deed was won, then trekked once more to Walsingham.
2 CONT
2 CONT Mr. Brown’s next venture was happier – a farm in Southwold, near Five Stakes – 40 acres cleared.   After her wanderings, those 17 prosperous years at Pain’s Mills must have seemed like heaven to Elizabeth Hoy Brown.   Good frame h
2 CONC ouse and barn, a new school, a Methodist meeting, out of debt.   But Brinton Paine Brown was again deciding they must move to a new land, for their nine sons and four daughters, to settle about the ????   Many neighbors having mov
2 CONC ed west to the States, Mr. Brown took a trip out there.   But, the Middle West was filled with ague.   Anyway, Brinton Paine Brown preferred forest land to open country.
2 CONT
2 CONT Then a friend, a hunter by the name of Crippen, mentioned Dereham.   "Beech and maple.   No better land.   Big block to be had cheap right now." – and that is how Elizabeth Brown was called upon to leave comfort near St. Thomas an
2 CONC d trek over those awful floating logs to the Brownsville cabin 100 years ago to the land...
2 CONT
2 CONT ...about the milk and honey...
2 CONT
2 CONT Brinton Paine Brown prospered mightily, became a landed proprietor with 1,600 acres of some of the richest land in Ontario, on which he settled his sons.   And never were there such sons to work and help.   Even little Walter and 
2 CONC Enoch, nine and 11 respectively, that first winter of 1841 – 42, measured off two acres on their own, cleared it, chopping logs from one to two feet through, in lengths, piling the brush, all in 13 days!   Until 1865, the Brown fa
2 CONC mily pooled labor, stock, implements, until the younger boys were all married settled or educated.
2 CONT
2 CONT Brinton Paine Brown was a man of decision in matters other than land.   He saw value in Christian neighbors, brought in this class about him at any sacrifice.   To secure John Loucks, Malahide, an "exhorter", the Browns took the 1
2 CONC 0 Loucks children in with their own 11, in their two-room cabin (one room downstairs, one up) until a Loucks cabin was erected.   It was the same with schools and churches which sprung up wherever this dynamic Brown went.   An ord
2 CONC ained Methodist minister, Brinton Paine Brown usually preached twice a Sunday, promoting incipient churches in the district, as well as one in his own community.   He preached at Goshen , North Bayham, Corinth.   Tradition claims 
2 CONC he was the first Methodist preacher in Tillsonburg.   He conducted many marriages.   Amasa Wood of St. Thomas was married by him.   And he was constantly in attendance at funerals far and near.
2 CONT
2 CONT He knew various ways to pay for land.   In a day when $1 was as hard to come by as is now $100, the Southwold farm was largely paid for by breeding, selling horses, breaking steers, for oxen and selling them.   Or he would burn 35
2 CONC ,000 to 50,000 bricks, sell them, and then hire out to build them into chimneys, ovens.   He could lath and plaster, build a sleight, fashion a yoke.   Then pioneer cash expenses of the large Brown family were infinitesimal, a tri
2 CONC fle for carding wool into rolls, "fulling" flannel for the boys’ suits, so much cash per child to the school teacher.   A shoemaker came once a year to peg footwear; the tanner cured the hides on shares.   They scorched beans and 
2 CONC peas for coffee; the maples gave them sugar and syrup; their biscuits were raised with saleratus from the lye of corn cob ash.   And yet the Browns were progressive.   The new cylinder threshing machine, grain separator – all adva
2 CONC nced equipment had only to prove their worth and they were bought.   Enoch Brown in his day became an internationally known cattle shipper.
2 CONT
2 CONT But the old pioneers knew little of such things.   To earn all, pay out the least you can, was the saving law of pioneer existence.   The struggle for balance between labor and capital, the still unsettled give and take rights of 
2 CONC social economy, all were to be nursed and bred by the hectic machine-age in the 100 years between them and 1942.
2 CONT
2 CONT The land taken up at Brownsville by Brinton Paine Brown, remained in the family for 100 years, being sold in 1942 to Aubrey Buchner, formerly clerk of Middleton Township, by Fred J. Brown, a grandson and the last occupant of the h
2 CONC omestead, upon his retirement to Tillsonburg. – Ed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Source: Tweedsmuir Histories – Oxford County Genealogical Society, Woodstock, Ontario, Canada. 
