1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/memorial/119058000/benjamin-franklin-eames
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Benjamin Franklin Eames
2 CONT BIRTH
2 CONT 21 Nov 1819
2 CONT Monroe County, New York, USA
2 CONT DEATH
2 CONT 26 Jan 1891 (aged 71)
2 CONT Spring Lake, Ottawa County, Michigan, USA
2 CONT BURIAL
2 CONT Spring Lake Cemetery
2 CONT Spring Lake, Ottawa County, Michigan, USA
2 CONT MEMORIAL ID
2 CONT 119058000 · View Source
2 CONT MEMORIAL
2 CONT PHOTOS 1
2 CONT FLOWERS 0
2 CONT Benjamin Eames was born in Mendon, New York on November 21, 1819 and moved to Milwaukee in October, 1844. On December 5, 1849 he went into business with John W. Luscomb in the manufacture of woodenware, locating first in Grand Rap
2 CONC ids and then moving to Ottawa Point [Ferrysburg], beginning business on April 10, 1850. Luscomb withdrew from the business around 1855, and Eames then constructed a new mill that utilized steam power. Benjamin and his family were 
2 CONC listed in the 1850 and 1860 censuses as residents of Spring Lake Township. In 1861 he sold to Willard and Bolles, who manufactured wooden pails. The next year Benjamin bought 23 acres in Section 15 of Spring Lake Township, on the 
2 CONC west shore of Spring Lake, where he planted 1,000 peach trees, 600 apple trees, and other fruit. Benjamin sold this land to Charles Soule in 1867 and bought 30 acres in Section 9, Spring Lake Township, also for the purpose of grow
2 CONC ing fruit. He and his wife, Charlotte, had a daughter Nora, who in 1876 married Thomas Johnston of the Johnston Boiler family. Charlotte lived from December 11, 1833 to March 20, 1886. Benjamin died on January 25, 1891. A son, Fre
2 CONC derick A., died accidentally on April 22, 1861 at the age of 13. Other children were Kate, born in 1861; Viola, born in 1863; and Frankie, born in 1871. All the children were born in Michigan. The family was buried at Spring Lake 
2 CONC Cemetery. [Industrial Hi-Lites, January, 1951.]
2 CONT Floyd S.
2 CONT * Daughter Viola married William Campbell and moved to Wisconsin.
2 CONT *Daughter Sonora married Thomas Johnston and died in Grand Haven in 1927.
2 CONT
