1 TITL Rev.
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} From C. W. Brooks, District Missionary of the Baptist  Missionary Convention at Watkins, N. Y., under date of  Sept. 10. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Dr. B. S. Swetland: — 
2 CONT
2 CONT I send you by this mail the concluding article of  the captivity of Luke Swetland, whose name is historic  in this vicinity, as will appear in the history of the "Sul-  livans Campaign" now in preparation by the editor of  the "Wa
2 CONC tkins Review." My grandmother was Mercy  Swetland, a daughter of J. Swetland, Jr., and niece of  Luke Swetland. Mercy married Amos Leanord, a  Revolutionary soldier. He died in 1821. These were  my mother's parents. I heard from m
2 CONC y grandmother's  lips the story of the war of the Revolution; the account  of her brother Benjamin, who was a soldier, and the  account of her Uncle Luke, who was the Seneca captive  referred to in history. My grandmother was note
2 CONC d for  her Christianity, kindness to the poor and for geniality  of temper. She died May 23, 1850. My mother's name  was Dorathy Stevens Leonard. She married Samuel  Brooks and died at the age of 88. There are now living  five of 
2 CONC us, Leander Brooks, Alfred Centre, N. Y.;  Orville L. Brooks, North Norwich, N. Y.; Charles W.  Brooks, Watkins, N. Y.; Sarah M, Phelps, Wilkesbarre,  Pa.; Elotia J. Brooks, Rath bun, Kansas City, Mo. -Also  two brothers who are d
2 CONC ead. Milton Brooks who died at  the age of 14 and Samuel Brooks, M. D. of Milford, Pa.,  who died at the age of 44. Leander and Samuel were  both soldiers at the war of the Rebellion. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Sincerely yours, 
2 CONT
2 CONT C. M. Brooks. [letter submitted to the Swetland Family Reunion 1st annual meeting in Brocton, NY, 1896]
