1 NAME Christopher Miner /Spencer/
2 GIVN Christopher Miner
2 _MARNM Spencer
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 20 JUN 1833
2 ADDR
3 CITY Manchester
3 STAE Connecticut
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 14 JAN 1922
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://www.findagrave.com/cgi-bin/fg.cgi?page=gr&GRid=24623998
2 CONT
2 CONT https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Christopher_Miner_Spencer
2 CONT
2 CONT Christopher Miner Spencer (June 20, 1833 – January 14, 1922) was an American inventor, from Manchester, Connecticut, who invented the Spencer repeating rifle, one of the earliest models of lever-action rifle, a steam powered "hors
2 CONC eless carriage", and several other inventions. He developed the first fully automatic turret lathe, which in its small- to medium-sized form is also known as a screw machine.
2 CONT
2 CONT Early years
2 CONT
2 CONT Spencer worked for Samuel Colt’s factory, where he learned the arms-making trade.
2 CONT
2 CONT Civil War
2 CONT
2 CONT Although the Spencer rifle had been developed as early as 1859, it was not initially used by the Union. On August 18, 1863, Christopher Spencer walked into the White House carrying one of his rifles and a supply of cartridges. He 
2 CONC walked past the sentries, and into Abraham Lincoln's office. After some discussion, he returned the following afternoon, when Spencer and Lincoln were joined by Edwin Stanton, Secretary of War and other officials, and the group th
2 CONC en proceeded to walk out on the Mall. Near the site of the Washington Monument, they engaged in target shooting.
2 CONT
2 CONT Subsequent to that meeting, the US ordered some 13,171 rifles and carbines, along with some 58 million rounds of ammunition. General Ulysses S. Grant declared Spencer rifles "the best breech-loading arms available". Total wartime 
2 CONC production approached 100,000 rifles. Many veterans took these rifles home with them after the war and their rifles saw widespread use on the western frontier. With so many military surplus rifles available, there was little post-
2 CONC war demand for new rifles; and Spencer was unable to recover investments made in manufacturing machinery. Spencer Repeating Rifle Company declaring bankruptcy in 1868; and assets were acquired by Oliver Winchester for $200,000 in 
2 CONC 1869.
2 CONT
2 CONT Post-Civil War
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1868, while at the Roper Repeating Arms Company in Amherst, Massachusetts, he worked with Charles E. Billings, and Sylvester H. Roper. After Roper's firearms company failed, and the following year, 1869, the Billings and Spence
2 CONC r founded a partnership in Hartford, Connecticut called Billings & Spencer, which would manufacture sewing machines, drop-forged hand tools, and machine tools.
2 CONT
2 CONT Around 1882, Spencer started a new company, the Spencer Arms Company, in Windsor, Connecticut. Its most remarkable product was likely the Spencer Pump-Action Shotgun. Produced between 1882 and 1889, this was the first commercially
2 CONC  successful slide-action (or pump-action) shotgun. Most were manufactured in 12-gauge with 10-gauge being an uncommon variant. Once again faced with financial hardships, Spencer's company and his patents were purchased circa 1890 
2 CONC by Francis Bannerman & Sons of New York who continued to manufacture his shotgun until around 1907.
