1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Walter Hoy Brown Mr. and Mrs. Walter H. Brown, Stanley street, celebrated their golden wedding on July 1. The only remaining children - four in number - of Rev. Brinton Paine Brown, who settled on the back street i
2 CONC n the township of Southwold in 1824, of whom Walter H. Brown was one, were present, and all are over 70 years of age. The average of those that sat down for lunch at one table (one young widow excepted) was 74 years. There were ni
2 CONC ne at this table whose united ages totaled 665 years. Of this number Rev. F. Berry who officiated at the wedding 50 years ago, and his wife, led, their united ages being 164. There was in the dining room, and still faithful in kee
2 CONC ping time, an English clock at least 200 years old, which had been the property of Mrs. W. H. Brown's grandfather." Transcribed by Mike Brown. The Sentinel Review, Saturday, January 7, 1905 The Browns of Brownsville Mr. Enoch B. B
2 CONC rown has issued two cat little pamphlets containing the ancestral history of his late father and mother, Brinton Paine Brown and Elizabeth Hoy Brown. Both were early pioneers of Oxford and the Brown family, from which the village 
2 CONC of Brownsville takes its name, has long been one of the most prominent in the southern part of the county. The record of Brinton Paine Brown begins about A. D. 1600 and continues until 1904 and includes his great grand children, a
2 CONC s also brothers and sisters and other relatives. The Paine family was long prominent in New England, many of its members occupied judicial and other public positions. General Edward Paine, the founder of Painsville, O., where was 
2 CONC erected a monument to him., which was unveiled on July 21st 1900, was one of hte family (this is correct - BCJ).Benjamin (sic - should be Benajah) Brown, who married Violetta Paine, was the father of Brinton Paine Brown and having
2 CONC , as he thought, suffereed injustice in the United States for whose independence he and his ancestors had fought, moved in 1797 from the Genesee Valley to Canada, settling first on the Grand River at Big Bend, now well known as Bo
2 CONC w Park farm, founded by the late Hon. George Brown. In 1801 they moved to West Oxford, settling on a farm on the River Thames two miles west of where Ingersol now stands. He was drowned in the winter of 1805, while crossing the ic
2 CONC e of Burlington Bay, having first heroically asked those who came to rescue him, to first rescue his companion, he being able, as he thought to save himself. Brinton Paine Brown, born August 1st, 1797, married on January 1st, 1817
2 CONC , (they married young in those days!) Elizabeth Hoy, the ceremony being performed by Squire John Backouse (sic Backhouse) at Walsingham. The union was blessed by twelve children, eight sons and four daughters, teh daughters being 
2 CONC Violetta, Marilla, Phoebe, Almyra and Lucy Ann, the sons Brinton Paine, Edward Foster, Enoch Burdick, and old county and township Councilor of Oxford, Walter Hoy, Benajah Mallery., Isaac Wesley, a physician at Beachville, Peter Jo
2 CONC hnson, a well known lawyer and politician at Ingersoll, and Jno. Addyman. When Brinton Paine Brown, senior settled at Brownsville in Dereham Township, he "took up" 1600 acres of land, which he afterwards divided among his sons. He
2 CONC  was all his life a prominent figure in the community and in his capacity as a regularly ordained minister married many couples and performed many funeral services in the southern part of the county. The history of the Brown Famil
2 CONC y is given in detail in these pamphlets, and has evidently been prepared with care. There are also excellent portraits of MR. and Mrs. Paine, Brown and of Enoch Brown. It constitutes valuable material for local history and local h
2 CONC istorians are under obligation to Mr. E. B. Brown for undertaking and publishing these records, which are not merely of personal but of general interest in this district. It is to be hoped that his example will be followed by the 
2 CONC representatives of other early pioneer families in Oxford County and elsewhere.
2 CONT
2 CONT The death occurred at Brownsville yesterday of Enoch B. Brown, in his 77th year. The deceased was widely known and was highly esteemed by a wide circle of friends. The funeral takes place from his late residence at 1:30 o'clock on
2 CONC  Wednesday afternoon and proceed to the Methodist church at Brownsville, thence to Delmer Cemetery." 179 Oxford County Surrogate Court. "~Will of Enoch Burdick Brown 	Oxford County Surrogate Court, 	Estate Files, 	Index 1901 - 193
2 CONC 0, 	Reel 1622, Estate Number 4571 	(All pages stamped as being filed September 9, 1907 with the name James Canfield, D.C., & C.C.C.O.)  	PETITION FOR PROBATE 	Unto the Surrogate Court 	of the County of Oxford  	The Petition of Mil
2 CONC ton Richard Brown, of the Village of Appin in the County of Middlesex, cheesemaker, Edwin Arthur Brown <eabrown.html> of the Township of Bayham in the County of Elgin Farmer, Fred Joseph Brown <fjbrown.html> of the Township of Der
2 CONC eham in the County of Oxford Farmer and Carrie Josephine Jackson of the Village of Union in the County of Elgin Married Woman.  	Humbly showeth that Enoch Burdick Brown late of the Township of Dereham in the County of Oxford Farme
2 CONC r, 	deceased, died on or about the 27th day of May at the Township of Dereham in the County of Oxford and that the said deceased at the time of his death had fixed place of abode at Township of Dereham in the County of Oxford.  	T
2 CONC hat the said deceased in his lifetime duly made his last Will and Testament bearing date the Fourteenth day of April in the year of our Lord one Thousand nine hundred and two." 180 Brown, Nicholas Carter, Diary of Nicholas Carter 
2 CONC Brown, 1855-1920. "Wed. 29 May 1907, "To my cousin Mr. Browns funeral aged 77 years."."
