1 NAME Caleb R. /Smith/
2 GIVN Caleb R.
2 SURN Smith
2 _MARNM Smith
2 NICK Ira?
1 SEX M
1 BURI
2 PLAC Quaker Cemetery
2 ADDR
3 CITY Knoxville
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 BIRT
2 DATE 1826
2 ADDR
3 CITY New York City
3 STAE New York
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE 18 JUL 1859
2 ADDR
3 STAE Pennsylvania
3 CTRY United States
1 OCCU Farmer
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} died same year as his father.
2 CONT
2 CONT abstract of will  431 	Smith, Caleb date of probate Aug. 5,1859 	Deerfield 	Exec. - Wife - Susan Smith Children mentioned, not named Wit. - J.W. Bellows, J.G. Seely, Enoch E. Bowen
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.joycetice.com/document/tcdockb2.htm
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT The Quaker Burying ground, now in Knoxville, is marked by a Historical Marker, simply-Quaker Burying Ground, 1817-1882. Was originally located in Deerfield Township since Knoxville was not incorporated until 1851. On April 24, 181
2 CONC 7, ZADOC BOWEN sold to JOSEPH COLVIN and GEORGE MARTIN of Troupsburg, N.Y., two acres of land for the sum of $50.00 to be used as a burying ground. Joseph Colvin and George Martin were acting as Trustees, appointed by the Society 
2 CONC of Friends in Deerfield, to secure land for Meeting House and burying ground. This land was located where Main and Seeley streets met, and the section used as burying ground is maintained by the Borough of Knoxville, today. Earlie
2 CONC st burials before 1819, just plain head stones were used. In 1820 - 1830, Friends were only allowed to use field stones with initials and year of death cut in. Family lots were not used, but bodies were placed in a row, as the die
2 CONC d. From 1840 - 1850, low plain carved stones were permitted. Earliest recorded date was that of Abigail, wife of Eleazer Clark, 1824. The most recent, Pearl Clark, 1882. Of the 57 burials recorded in 1956, those of Bowen, Clark, H
2 CONC owland, Pease and Seely played a role in Deerfield History.
2 CONT
2 CONT The tombstones have been removed and are piled at the rear of the lot. It has a park like look with settings of flowers and shrubbery.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Wellsboro Chapter of D.A.R. have placed bronze markers and flags by the Quaker Cemetery plaque, honoring Revolutionary Soldiers buried here.
2 CONT
2 CONT Please see Joyce Tice, Quaker Cemetery, Knoxville, Tioga County, PA.
2 CONT http://www.joycetice.com/cemt/quaker.htm
