1 NAME Manin /Livermore/
2 GIVN Manin
2 SURN Livermore
2 _MARNM Livermore
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE 18 SEP 1810
2 ADDR
3 CITY Brookfield
3 STAE New York
1 OCCU Tailor
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Adventures of a tailor:
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of fourteen he moved to Independence, NY; as he was small in stature and of a not very strong constitution he began to learn the trade of a tailor in Hornellsville, N.Y., and finished it in Danesville. He after returned
2 CONC  to Hornellsville.   
2 CONT
2 CONT He went to Dresden, N.Y., and worked with his  cousin Fidus Livermore.  
2 CONT
2 CONT From Dresden, he traveled on foot to Buffalo and to Youngstown, N.Y..  He crossed over into Canada, working at his trade in several towns, but not liking the severe winters he determined to go South.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took a boat at Port Stanley and crossed Lake Erie, landing in Cleveland, Ohio.  While there had a very severe attack of yellow fever, which caused nearly all his hair to come out, leaving him very bald.
2 CONT
2 CONT He then went to Cincinnati and thence by boat down the Ohio and Mississippi Rivers to New Orleans.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He remained in the South sixteen years, during which he crossed over the Cumberland mountains from Georgia to Kentucky, on foot. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Owing to a defective mail service, his family had given him up for dead. By chance, one of his brothers picked up a Southern paper in which Manin had an advertisement of his business.  His brother immediately wrote Manin a letter,
2 CONC  who was glad enough to hear from home, and shortly after came North to Independence, where he settled.  He built himself a house and in a short time was married.  He moved to Alfred, in 1870, where he was living when last heard f
2 CONC rom.  He is a member of the Seventh Day Baptist Church.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT Adventures of a tailor:
2 CONT
2 CONT At the age of fourteen he moved to Independence, NY; as he was small in stature and of a not very strong constitution he began to learn the trade of a tailor in Hornellsville, N.Y., and finished it in Danesville. He after returned
2 CONC  to Hornellsville.   
2 CONT
2 CONT He went to Dresden, N.Y., and worked with his  cousin Fidus Livermore.  
2 CONT
2 CONT From Dresden, he traveled on foot to Buffalo and to Youngstown, N.Y..  He crossed over into Canada, working at his trade in several towns, but not liking the severe winters he determined to go South.
2 CONT
2 CONT He took a boat at Port Stanley and crossed Lake Erie, landing in Cleveland, Ohio.  While there had a very severe attack of yellow fever, which caused nearly all his hair to come out, leaving him very bald.
2 CONT
2 CONT He then went to Cincinnati and thence by boat down the Ohio and Mississippi Rivers to New Orleans.  
2 CONT
2 CONT He remained in the South sixteen years, during which he crossed over the Cumberland mountains from Georgia to Kentucky, on foot. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Owing to a defective mail service, his family had given him up for dead. By chance, one of his brothers picked up a Southern paper in which Manin had an advertisement of his business.  His brother immediately wrote Manin a letter,
2 CONC  who was glad enough to hear from home, and shortly after came North to Independence, where he settled.  He built himself a house and in a short time was married.  He moved to Alfred, in 1870, where he was living when last heard f
2 CONC rom.  He is a member of the Seventh Day Baptist Church.
