1 NAME Percival Hopkins /Hollister Spencer/
2 GIVN Percival Hopkins
2 _MARNM Hollister Spencer
1 NAME Percival H. Spencer
1 SEX M
1 BIRT
2 DATE ABT 3 APR 1897
2 ADDR
3 CITY Windsor
3 STAE CT
3 CTRY United States
1 DEAT
2 DATE ABT 1995
2 ADDR
3 CITY Torrance
3 STAE California
3 CTRY United States
1 NOTE {geni:about_me} https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Percival_H._Spencer
2 CONT
2 CONT Percival Hopkins Spencer (April 30, 1897 – January 16, 1995) was an American inventor, aviation pioneer, test pilot and businessman.
2 CONT
2 CONT Biography
2 CONT
2 CONT He was born on April 30, 1897 to Christopher Miner Spencer (1833-1922), the inventor of, among other things, the Spencer repeating rifle.
2 CONT
2 CONT The then 14-year-old Spencer built his first pontooned hang glider in April 1911 from plans he found in a Popular Mechanics magazine. On May 15, 1914, Spencer made his first powered flight in a Curtiss flying boat. In 1929, he bro
2 CONC ke the light airplane altitude record—18,571 ft (5,660 m)—piloting a Curtiss Robin monoplane. Spencer was the president of Amphibians Inc. for three years selling amphibian designs.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1937, he joined Sikorsky engineer Victor A. Larsen to form the Spencer-Larsen Aircraft Corporation and design their first, and only, amphibious aircraft, the Spencer-Larsen SL-12C. Development of the plane progressed slowly and
2 CONC  in September 1940, Spencer left the partnership to form his own company, the Spencer Aircraft Company.
2 CONT
2 CONT His resulting design was the Spencer S-12 Air Car Amphibian. Construction began on March 1, 1941. The small, two-seat S-12 prototype, registration NX29098, made its first flight on August 8, 1941. The S-12 was a fabric-covered amp
2 CONC hibian with a unique boxlike forward cabin, a high wing with a two-bladed propeller in pusher configuration and a long, slender tail boom.
2 CONT
2 CONT In December 1941, Spencer put the Air Car into storage and joined the war effort as a test pilot for the Republic Aircraft Corporation. By 1943, he had flight tested 134 of the company's P-47 Thunderbolts.
2 CONT
2 CONT In April 1943, Spencer left Republic Aircraft for the Mills Novelty Company of Chicago, Illinois, which wanted to use his Air Car to promote the company. Spencer used the company's wood forming equipment to build a new egg-shaped 
2 CONC cabin for the Air Car and began demonstrating the aircraft to his former employers, Republic Aircraft. Seeing the potential of the Air Car as the perfect sports plane for pilots returning from the war, Republic purchased the right
2 CONC s to the Air Car in December 1943 and immediately began development of an all-metal version designated the Model RC-1 Thunderbolt Amphibian. On November 30, 1944, the first RC-1 Thunderbolt Amphibian, registered NX41816, made its 
2 CONC first flight with Spencer at the controls.[citation needed] The design led to the Republic RC-3 Seabee. On November 22, 1945, the prototype RC-3 (NX87451) came off the assembly line at Republic's factory in Farmingdale, New York, 
2 CONC and on December 1 made its first flight in Farmingdale with Spencer at the controls.
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1968, Spencer and retired United States Air Force Colonel Dale L. "Andy" Anderson formed a new company to market a four-place amphibious home built design once again called the Spencer Amphibian Air Car, with S-12C, S-12D, and 
2 CONC S-12E, and S-14 variants. The first example was test flown in 1970 in Chino, California. The unit had a build cost of $8700.00. The S-12-D Aircar was an improved and larger four-seat aircraft, which retains the basic layout of the
2 CONC  Seabee. The two-seat S-14 used advanced composites and was test flown by Spencer in 1983 at the age of 86. Its design did not meet expectations and the sole example was donated to the Experimental Aircraft Association Museum in O
2 CONC shkosh, Wisconsin.
2 CONT
2 CONT Spencer died in a nursing home in Torrance, California in 1995 at age 97.
